  "id": "Compound::DB01024 - Disease::DOID:418",
  "verbalization": "Mycophenolic Acid is implicated in the pathogenesis of Systemic Scleroderma through a chain of molecular interactions involving Mycophenolate Mofetil, a metabolite that acts as an Inosine Monophosphate Dehydrogenase Inhibitor. This mechanism suggests that Mycophenolic Acid may contribute to the development of Systemic Scleroderma by inducing metabolic disturbances that affect cellular function. The shared pathway involving Mycophenolate Mofetil and the Phenols indicates a potential role in modulating inflammatory or fibrotic processes. The evidence from the three paths highlights a consistent link between Mycophenolic Acid and Systemic Scleroderma through its metabolite, reinforcing the hypothesis that the drug's effects may be mediated via this biochemical route. The presence of a common ancestor in the Inosine Monophosphate Dehydrogenase Inhibitor class further supports the notion of a shared biological mechanism, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
